Ilkyne was a terrestrial planet like [[Geography/Althane/index|Althane]], slightly larger, and Althane's twin. It had much harsher weather patterns than most of Althane and was covered near-completely in dense cloud cover, making mapping the surface remotely impossible. Bursts of red lightning could be seen within the clouds, and a great shadow could sometimes be seen moving on the surface.

Beneath the cloud lay the City of Yellow, the seat of [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/The Deep|the Deep]]. It was built as concentric rings around a hill. The streets were paved in interlocking triangles, squares and hexagons, split by fissures that leaked yellow light, and the buildings were faced in stone like marble and roofed in gold tiles. The air was dry and acrid and smelled of rotten eggs, and a low organ note ran under everything, louder and clearer toward the hill. A long staircase climbed from the outermost ring to the top of the hill, and where it crested there was no lighthouse to be seen: a great organ grown out of fungus blocked the way, and beyond it, in a depression at the hill's top, a lake of water so deep a blue it was nearly black held a lighthouse in its middle.

Six black pyramids stood spaced around the foot of the hill, their faces carved with spiralling yellow cracks, each with a plaque that named it in a script a reader understood without having learned it: Origin, Sky, Cycle, Geometry, History and Hierarchy. None showed a door. Each was a shaping point: touching a plaque opened a trial in which the city fell away and a room or an arena grew around the plaza, and passing the trial sent a beam of yellow light from the spire and brought another part of the city back whole. As the spires were lit the city stopped half-melting into itself, its endless stairways and crossing buildings settling into a plain and solid shape, and it grew out past its rings and filled with people. It spread further over the planet since, and held tens of thousands of them.

The city's people were hunched figures wrapped in yellow burlap, shuffling through the streets, scratching at the walls and pointing at strangers, each trailing a cord that led up toward the hill. As the spires were lit they grew varied and purposeful, taking up trades, walking in pairs and threes, and dressing in frilled and coloured clothes set with gems; some in burlap remained only in the alleys. They bowed with one arm back and one arm tucked under, and kept no bank. They offered a guest nothing, saying no food they could serve would be good enough, and sent anyone who wanted something better up the hill to the palace for ambrosia and nectar. Their true form was a smooth gray body with three legs and three arms set radial around the torso and no face.

It was destroyed at the end of the Deep's work. When [[Org/Althane/The Weft|the Weft]] killed the Deep, the artificial moon of [[Geography/Althane/The Thirteenth Realm|the Thirteenth Realm]] fell on the City of Yellow and wiped it off the planet, and the Thirteenth Realm itself slammed into Ilkyne; there were not enough whole pieces of either left to carry away. Ilkyne is gone from Althane's sky, and the twelve realms now orbit Althane alone.
